From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joshua Doll <brektyme@brektyme.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 16:50:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: Add device links for Apple T2 Ice Lake NHI

tb_apple_add_links() only matches the discrete Thunderbolt controllers
(Light Ridge, Cactus Ridge, Falcon Ridge), where the NHI sits behind a
PCIe switch and the tunneled downstream ports can be found by walking
upstream. Ice Lake integrates the controller into the SoC: the NHI and
the PCIe root ports it tunnels are siblings on the root bus, so the
upstream walk finds nothing and the function bails out.

tb_acpi_add_links() does not help either, because Apple firmware does not
describe the tunneled ports the way it expects. Both paths therefore fail
on T2 Macs and tb_probe() complains:

  thunderbolt 0000:00:0d.2: device links to tunneled native ports are missing!

Without those links there is no PM dependency between the NHI and the
PCIe root ports it tunnels, so on resume the tunneled PCIe and USB devices
can be resumed before the NHI has reestablished their tunnels. Observed on
a MacBookPro16,2 with a Thunderbolt dock attached: long stalls with xHCI
command timeouts during suspend, and the tunneled devices unresponsive
after resume.

Match the Ice Lake root ports by PCI ID on the root bus instead, and add
the device links from there.

The port to NHI assignment follows the upstream "thunderbolt: Add device
links" series: NHI0 tunnels 0x8a1d and 0x8a1f, NHI1 tunnels 0x8a21 and
0x8a23.

Tested on MacBookPro16,2 (kernel 7.2.6): the warning is gone and all four
Thunderbolt root ports gain a supplier link to their NHI.

Signed-off-by: Joshua Doll <brektyme@brektyme.com>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c | 48 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 48 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/tb.c
@@ -3302,6 +3302,54 @@
 	.disconnect_xdomain_paths = tb_disconnect_xdomain_paths,
 };
 
+static bool tb_add_nhi_link(struct tb_nhi *nhi, struct pci_dev *pdev)
+{
+	const struct device_link *link;
+
+	link = device_link_add(&pdev->dev, nhi->dev,
+			       DL_FLAG_AUTOREMOVE_SUPPLIER | DL_FLAG_PM_RUNTIME);
+	if (!link) {
+		dev_warn(nhi->dev, "device link creation from %s failed\n",
+			 dev_name(&pdev->dev));
+		return false;
+	}
+
+	dev_dbg(nhi->dev, "created link from %s\n", dev_name(&pdev->dev));
+	return true;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Ice Lake integrates the Thunderbolt controller into the SoC, so the NHI and
+ * the PCIe root ports it tunnels are siblings on the root bus instead of
+ * sitting below a common upstream switch port. Match the root ports by PCI ID
+ * rather than walking upstream.
+ */
+static bool tb_icl_add_links(struct tb_nhi *nhi)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *nhi_pdev = to_pci_dev(nhi->dev);
+	struct pci_bus *bus = nhi_pdev->bus;
+	struct pci_dev *pdev;
+	bool ret = false;
+
+	if (!pci_is_root_bus(bus))
+		return false;
+
+	for_each_pci_bridge(pdev, bus) {
+		switch (nhi_pdev->device) {
+		case PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_ICL_NHI0:
+			if (pdev->device == 0x8a1d || pdev->device == 0x8a1f)
+				ret |= tb_add_nhi_link(nhi, pdev);
+			break;
+		case PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_ICL_NHI1:
+			if (pdev->device == 0x8a21 || pdev->device == 0x8a23)
+				ret |= tb_add_nhi_link(nhi, pdev);
+			break;
+		}
+	}
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
 /*
  * During suspend the Thunderbolt controller is reset and all PCIe
  * tunnels are lost. The NHI driver will try to reestablish all tunnels
@@ -3324,6 +3372,9 @@
 	case PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_FALCON_RIDGE_2C_NHI:
 	case PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_FALCON_RIDGE_4C_NHI:
 		break;
+	case PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_ICL_NHI0:
+	case PCI_DEVICE_ID_INTEL_ICL_NHI1:
+		return tb_icl_add_links(nhi);
 	default:
 		return false;
 	}
-- 
2.55.0

